message: "If you cite this computational research thesis, please use the metadata below. No DOI is minted here; do not invent one."
type: thesis
title: "In-silico prioritisation of phytochemical effects on mitochondrial membrane potential and metabolic-regulator binding under claim–evidence gates"
authors:
  - family-names: Ogbonna
    given-names: Kelechi Emeka
    email: kelechiogbonna300@gmail.com
    alias: cloudynirvana
abstract: >-
  Mitochondrial-reprogramming roadmaps prioritise simulating membrane
  potential and AMPK/PI3K/GLUT1 binding, but docking scores and tip-shift
  anecdotes are easily smuggled into kinetic parameters. A frozen twelve-row
  toy library is scored with a declared linear surrogate, not with a docking
  engine. Claim level stops at a nomination for a future observation channel.
  Promotion of a column-winning score into a kinetic rate, and promotion of a
  roadmap G_tip anecdote, both return refused, and the parameter hash does
  not change. A separate kinetic schedule has Fisher rank 4 of 4 and does not
  read the scores. Not a medical device.
keywords:
  - mitochondrial membrane potential
  - AMPK
  - PI3K
  - GLUT1
  - surrogate score
